    "description": "Northstar operates a browser application, a mobile API, and a service-to-service endpoint behind one gateway. Browser requests carry a server-side session cookie, mobile requests carry a signed OIDC token, and service requests use mTLS client certificates. The gateway's Edge Security component validates each credential, while controllers, tenant filters, and audit interceptors must consume the same stable user identifier, tenant identifier, roles, and authentication time. None of those downstream components may parse tokens, read certificate fields, or depend on the credential format. Expired, malformed, incorrectly addressed, and untrusted credentials must all produce a consistent denial without exposing which verification step failed to the caller. A contractor who appears in both the corporate directory and the customer identity provider must still resolve to one acting account. The engineering decision is where to establish this credential-independent identity representation and how to ensure every entry path produces it before request handling begins.",
    "decision_statement": "Decide whether to establish one normalized acting-user representation at the trust boundary so all downstream authorization, tenant filtering, and audit code consume identical identity attributes regardless of credential format.",
    "notes": "Cookie, token, and mTLS paths converge on one credential-independent acting-user representation (subject-descriptor); security-context is the near-miss for propagating it downstream, with verification (authenticator) as the supporting upstream boundary."
  },
